## Reflection

<details style="margin-top: 12px; border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden;">
  <summary style="background-color: #E4E2F8; padding: 12px 16px; cursor: pointer; font-weight: bold; color: #1F1976; font-size: 16px;">
    &#128161; Unit 1 was framed as "weak alignment" to AI-901. Unit 2 isn't. How might that change how you pace or emphasize content in this unit?
  </summary>
  <div style="background-color: #FFFFFF; padding: 16px; border: 1px solid #ACA8EA; font-size: 16px; color: #1A1A1A; line-height: 1.6;">
    There's no single right answer, but one reasonable response: since this unit's vocabulary and concepts carry real exam weight, it's worth making sure students can name and distinguish ideas precisely, regression versus classification, accuracy versus precision, not just demonstrate working code. A student who builds a working classifier but can't articulate why it's a classifier rather than a regression model has a gap this unit specifically can't afford to leave unaddressed.
  </div>
</details>

